ut if none exists, then we must find the **best attacking move**.\n\nRe-reading instruction: "Find a winning move that is legally valid" — but if no winning move exists, we should find the best move.\n\nAlso, "Winning Moves: Can you create a 4-in-a-row or open-3?"\n\nSo even if not immediate win, creating an open 3 or 4 is key.\n\nBest move: **G9** or **J9**?\n\n- J9: creates H9-I9-J9 = 3 horizontal, open on left (G9) and right (K9)\n- G9: creates G9-H9-I9 = 3 horizontal, open on both ends\n\nSymmetric.\n\nBut **G9** is better because:\n- It is less expected\n- It allows future play toward F9, which is farther from White\'s cluster\n- White has J8=O, which is closer to J9, so J9 might be more easily blocked\n\nAlso, playing G9 threatens to extend right, while White has no stone on G8 or G10 yet.\n\nMoreover, **G8** is empty — so vertical not strong, but horizontal is.\n\nAnother option: **I10**  \n- Creates vertical I8-I9-I10 = 3, but I7 is blocked by nothing? I7 is empty, but I6=O, so I7-I8-I9-I10 = 4 if I7 is played, but I6=O blocks above, so only open downward.\n\nSo I10 creates a **half-open 3**, worse than open 3.\n\nThus, **G9** and **J9** are better.\n\nNow, between G9 and J9, which is better?\n\n- The black stones are at H8, I8, H9, I9 — center of mass is H8-I8-H9-I9 square.\n- Expanding horizontally to the left (G9) or right (J9)?\n- Right is partially blocked: J8=O, which is directly below J9. That gives White a presence there.\n- Left side: G8 is empty, G7 empty, no White nearby.\n\nSo **G9 is safer and opens more space**.\n\nAdditionally, playing G9 sets up a potential **ladder breaker** or **fork** with the diagonal.\n\nTherefore, **G9 is the best move**.\n\nIs G9 occupied?  \nOccupied list: I6, H7, H8, I8, J8, H9, I9, H10  \nG9 is not in the list → valid.\n\nFinal decision:\n\nEvaluation: The strongest move is G9, creating an open three in row 9 (G9-H9-I9) with extension potential on both ends, while avoiding White\'s proximity on the right side (J8). It also supports a diagonal threat along the anti-diagonal (G9-H8). Alternative moves like J9 or I10 offer less strategic advantage due to White\'s nearby stones.\nReason: G9 maximizes offensive potential by forming an open three in a clear direction with minimal opposition, leveraging the existing cluster to build toward a future five-in-a-row.\nAnswer: {"col": "G", "row": 9}'}
